Italian officials now say at least 39  people died in the collapse of a giant  highway bridge a rescue effort continued  around the clock with searchers looking  for more survivors Charlie d'Agata is at  the scene in Genoa Italy where the  Morandi bridge fell leaving a massive  gap in that highway charlie good morning  good morning well those rescue efforts  are continuing beneath the bridge even  if the hope of finding survivors is  dimming government officials have  arrived here this morning they're facing  growing demands to explain exactly how  something like this could have happened  residents awoke to a new skyline and a  new reality the bridge that suddenly  ceased to exist taking so many lives  with it rescuers with sniffer dogs  searched through the night in a  desperate hope that somewhere buried  beneath the huge slabs of concrete  someone had managed to survive during a  heavy rainstorm the bridge suddenly  plunged a hundred fifty feet down into a  riverbed buildings and city streets  taking dozens of vehicles and those  inside with it  Diaby DiGiorgio shot that cellphone  footage they captured the moment I this  morning you wake up it's a dream call a  bad dream but it's not a very  the collapse of what has now been called  the bridge of death left a gaping hole  in the heart of this city and unanswered  questions about whether this structure  that local residents say was under a  constant state of repair was still fit  to do the job a half-century after it  was built  60,000 vehicles crossed the Morandi  bridge every day families on summer  vacations employees on their commute  truckers ferrying goods from one side of  the city to the other victims who but  for a few seconds on that bridge either  way would be alive today the public  prosecutor has already announced that a  criminal investigation will be underway  to determine whether negligence was  involved but locals here have been  expressing fears for years that that  bridge would never survive an earthquake  gaol and then seconds met the difference  between life and death thank you Charlie  d'Agata reporting from Genoa Italy today
 

